\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The two zero lines of the unperturbed wall}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Take spacing #{a=1}, periods #{2m,4} with #{m\ge2} even, and the two equal strips of \ref{fdip-01B4}. In the \vocabk{domain-wall operator}{fdip-01B3} put #{M_A=1/3}, #{M_B=5/2}, with one scalar fiber and a minimal complex spin module as permitted in the \vocabk{Wilson kernel}{fdip-0153}. Write #{E_x,E_y} for its Clifford directions, #{\Gamma=\ui E_xE_y} and #{P_\pm=(\bbI\pm E_x)/2}. At identity links the entire kernel consists of one line of each normal sign in the constant transverse character. For normalized normal amplitudes #{u,v} in the respective lines and adjacent columns #{q,q+1} of #{A},
##{|u_{q+1}|=\tfrac32|u_q|>0,\qquad |v_{q+1}|=\tfrac23|v_q|>0.}}

\proof{
\p{To include changes of transverse links later, give them phase #{\ue^{\ui \beta_x}} constant along each column, keeping normal links trivial. In transverse character #{p=2\pi j/4} their forward shift acts by #{\ue^{\ui (p-\beta_x)}} in the [covariant shift](fdip-009O) convention. Let #{T} be the increasing normal shift, #{b=p-\beta_x}, and #{d=2-M(x)-\cos b}, acting by multiplication. The operator on this character is
##{H_p=-E_x\sin b+\Gamma(d-P_+T^\dagger-P_-T).}
Indeed the centered transverse term before grading is #{\ui E_y\sin b}, with #{\ui\Gamma E_y=-E_x}. The two normal hops before grading are #{-P_+T^\dagger} and #{-P_-T}. The second term of #{H_p} is self-adjoint and exchanges the normal sign ranges.}

\p{At identity links the sine is constant, so #{H_p^2\ge\sin^2p\,\bbI}. At #{p=0,\pi} the sine vanishes, and the two kernel equations read #{u_{x-1}=d(x)u_x}, #{v_{x+1}=d(x)v_x}. Each nonzero initial amplitude goes around the circle exactly when #{\prod_x d(x)=1}. At #{p=\pi} this product is #{(8/3)^m(1/2)^m=(4/3)^m}. At #{p=0} it is #{(2/3)^m(-3/2)^m=1}. Every factor is nonzero, and each normal sign range in the spin module has dimension one. Within #{A} the factor #{d=2/3} gives the asserted ratios.}
}
